From 72d604a13f199ffcaf41494dd3a03cab887dbb69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 08:17:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] feat(logs): keep provider payloads on a retention you
 set in API Logs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Each upstream attempt already stored the provider's own response
(usage_events.raw_usage_json), and each video job its last poll payload —
the material that actually explains a failure — but nothing ever showed
them, and nothing ever cleared them either.

The detail modal now shows the payload per attempt behind a disclosure, and
the single-request CSV carries it (capped at 8000 characters per attempt;
the modal has the whole thing). Because that is also the bulkiest content in
these tables, and a provider can echo a prompt back inside it, its lifetime
is the operator's choice: a "Keep provider payloads N days" control sits in
the API Logs toolbar in the same button style as Filter and Export, bounded
to 1–365 days and gated by the admin write lock.

Saving applies immediately — shortening the window clears what it just
excluded rather than waiting for the nightly run — and writes a security
audit entry with what was purged. A daily job (04:10, beside the chat
retention run) does the routine sweep. Purging nulls the payload column and
keeps the row, so costs, tokens, status and the failure reason stay for as
long as the request log does.

